th='At harvest time, farmers thank the Sun, the Rain, the Earth and the Creator with songs, garlands and their labour – gratitude to nature and God.',
sum='“Harvest Hymn” is a song of thanksgiving sung by villagers after a rich harvest. It is arranged in four stanzas sung by different voices. The men first praise Surya, the generous lord of the morning, whose warm light ripened their crops, and offer songs, garlands and the golden produce of their fields with cymbal and flute. Next they praise Varuna, the merciful lord of the rain and sea, who sent rain and dew to nurture their furrows and grain. The women then praise Prithvi, the Earth, the all-powerful mother who feeds everyone and from whose womb all riches are born. Finally all voices together praise Brahma, the eternal Lord of the Universe, who is both the seed (beginning) and the scythe (end) of the harvest, and who lives in their hands, hearts and homes; they offer their lives and labour and ask for his help, guidance and care. The poem uses archaic words, repetition, alliteration, metaphor, personification and imagery to create a joyful, reverent rural festival mood.',
te='ఈ గీతం పంట కోతల తర్వాత రైతులు కృతజ్ఞతగా పాడే ప్రార్థన. మొదట పురుషులు సూర్యుని (పంటను పండించే వెలుగు) స్తుతిస్తారు; తర్వాత వరుణుని (వర్షం, మంచు ఇచ్చి నాగటి చాళ్ళను, ధాన్యాన్ని పోషించినవాడు) స్తుతిస్తారు. స్త్రీలు భూమాత పృథ్విని (అందరికీ అన్నం పెట్టే సర్వశక్తిమంతమైన తల్లి) స్తుతిస్తారు. చివరగా అందరూ కలిసి సృష్టికర్త బ్రహ్మను – పంటకు విత్తనమూ (ఆరంభం), కొడవలీ (ముగింపు) నీవే, మా చేతులు, హృదయం, ఇల్లు నీవే – అని స్తుతించి, తమ జీవితాలను, శ్రమను అర్పించి సహాయం, సలహా, రక్షణ కోరుతారు. తాళాలు, వేణువు, డోలుతో పాడే ఆనందోత్సవ వాతావరణం.',
pts=['Poet – Sarojini Naidu (1879–1949), the “Nightingale of India”, poet and freedom fighter.',
 'A hymn = a song of praise; this is a thanksgiving song at harvest time.',
 'Four parts: Men’s voices (Surya, Varuna), Women’s voices (Prithvi), All voices (Brahma).',
 'Surya – “lord of the lotus”, “munificent lord of the morn”; his mellowing radiance ripens the crops.',
 'Varuna – “lord of the rainbow”, “lord of the main (sea)”; his mercy cherished the furrows and fostered the grain.',
 'Prithvi – “queen of the gourd-flower”, “omnipotent mother”; she feeds all and gives birth to all riches.',
 'Brahma – “Lord of the Universe”, “ineffable Om”; “the Seed and the Scythe” – beginning and end of the harvest.',
 'Offerings – songs, garlands, the gold of fields and fruit, thanks, love, lives and labours.',
 'Musical instruments – cymbal and flute, cymbal and pipe, cymbal and drum, cymbal and prayer – joy and celebration.',
 'Poetic devices – alliteration (lives and our labours), repetition/anaphora (Thine is…), metaphor (Seed and Scythe), personification (Earth as mother), imagery, symbolism, allusion.',
 'Archaic words – thou, thee, thy, thine, art.',
 'Theme – gratitude to nature and God; nature and farming are closely connected.'],
