SEC = [
 (202, 'Before we read – I', [
  '1. In our region (Andhra Pradesh) the harvest festival is Sankranti, celebrated in mid-January (Bhogi, Makara Sankranti, Kanuma). We light bhogi fires, draw muggulu, worship cattle, fly kites, wear new clothes and prepare pongali and ariselu.',
  '2. (i) We offer a hymn to God/the divine (also to nature – Sun, Earth). (ii) We sing hymns during prayers, festivals and special occasions – at temples, churches, homes and school assemblies. (iii) We offer it to show gratitude, respect and devotion and to seek blessings.']),
 (203, 'Before we read – II, III', [
  'II. 1. thou – (iii) you (subject) 2. art – (iv) are 3. thee – (ii) you (object) 4. thy – (v) your 5. thine – (i) yours',
  'III. 1. radiance – (i) glow 2. tribute – (i) respect 3. cherished – (ii) loved 4. counsel – (i) advice 5. munificent – (ii) generous']),
 (206, 'Let us discuss – I, II', [
  'I. 1. Stanza 2 (Varuna – mercy that cherished the furrows and fostered the grain) 2. Stanza 1 (Surya – bright and munificent lord, bounty that prospered the sowing) 3. Stanza 4 (All Voices – lives and labours for tribute, succour, counsel, care) 4. Stanza 3 (Women – praise of Prithvi, the Earth)',
  'II. 1. harvest 2. nature and the Lord 3. God resides in them']),
 (207, 'Let us discuss – III–VI', [
  'III. “Bright and munificent lord of the morn” – alliteration (repeated ‘m’ sound: munificent, morn); “We bring thee our lives and our labours for tribute” – alliteration (repeated ‘l’ sound: lives, labours).',
  'IV. 1. sweet and omnipotent mother – (iii) – personification 2. The wealth of our valleys, new-garnered and ripe – (i) – imagery 3. Thou art the Seed and the Scythe – (ii) – metaphor',
  'V. Repetitions: “Lord of the lotus, lord of the harvest”; “Thine is the bounty… Thine is the bounty”; “Thine is the mercy… Thine is the mercy”; “our songs and our garlands”; “we hail thee… We praise thee”; “Lord of the Universe, Lord of our being”; “our Hands and our Heart and our Home”; “O Life of all life”. Purpose: to emphasise gratitude and praise, to create rhythm and music like a song/chant, and to make the hymn easy to sing together.',
  'VI. 1. Prithvi – (iii) fertile land, motherhood, nurturing 2. Surya – (i) light, energy, life 3. Varuna – (ii) mercy and nourishment']),
 (208, 'Let us think and reflect – Extract 1', [
  '(i) B. They have a deep sense of reverence and gratitude towards the divine.',
  '(ii) The word ‘gold’ symbolises the rich, ripe, golden harvest – the precious wealth of grain and fruit.',
  '(iii) It shows that the Sun’s gentle warm light ripens and softens the crops and fruits; without the Sun there would be no harvest.',
  '(iv) joy and celebration']),
 (208, 'Let us think and reflect – Extract 2', [
  '(i) B. Our entire existence',
  '(ii) True',
  '(iii) A. Father eternal B. ineffable',
  '(iv) The heading says “All Voices”, so men and women sing these lines together as a chorus.']),
 (209, 'Let us think and reflect – II (1–6)', [
  '1. Varuna, the God of rain, sent rain and dew that cherished their furrows and fostered (nurtured) their grain; without rain no crop can grow.',
  '2. The ripe, newly gathered harvest of grain from the valleys is called the “wealth of our valleys”, because crops are the farmers’ real wealth and source of life.',
  '3. The Earth gives them many kinds of joy – food, crops, flowers, fruits and all riches come from her.',
  '4. Their work in the fields is their most sincere offering; they believe God works through their hands, so they dedicate their labour to him with gratitude.',
  '5. To show that the whole community – men, women and everyone together – takes part in the harvest and in thanking God; it makes the hymn a group chorus like a real festival song.',
  '6. Through images of golden fields and fruit, garlands, songs, music of cymbal, flute, pipe and drum, ripe grain in the valleys, and the joyful voices of men and women praising together.']),
 (209, 'Let us think and reflect – II (7–12)', [
  '7. A hymn is a song of praise to God. The farmers are praising and thanking the gods of nature and the Creator for the harvest, so the hymn form suits the feeling of devotion and gratitude.',
  '8. (Sample) For Sankranti I would clean and decorate my house, draw muggulu, worship the cattle, offer the first grain to God, share sweets like ariselu with neighbours, fly kites and thank farmers.',
  '9. Surya gives light and warmth to ripen the crops; Varuna gives rain and dew to water the fields and grain; Prithvi gives fertile soil that feeds the plants and produces food.',
  '10. The poet shows that each step of farming depends on nature – the Sun ripens, the rain nurtures, the Earth feeds – and God is the seed and the scythe. Farmers thank nature for every harvest.',
  '11. Speech (sample): Dear friends, today our fields are full of golden grain. I thank the Sun for his warm light, the Rain for filling our tanks and canals, and Mother Earth for her rich soil. Our hard work alone could not do this. Let us protect our soil, water and trees so that nature keeps blessing us. Thank you.',
  '12. Dialogue (sample): Sun: I give my light and warmth so the plants can make food. | Rain: And I give water; without me the seeds cannot sprout. | Earth: I hold the roots and give minerals from my soil. | Sun: We each have a part. | Rain: Yes, when we work together, the farmer gets a good harvest. | Earth: Then let us bless the fields every year.']),
 (210, 'Let us learn – I (Farming implements)', [
  '1. (picture of sickle) – (iii) – SICKLE',
  '2. (picture of scythe) – (vi) – SCYTHE',
  '3. (picture of plough) – (iv) – PLOUGH',
  '4. (picture of trowel) – (ii) – TROWEL',
  '5. (picture of rake) – (i) – RAKE',
  '6. (picture of spade) – (v) – SPADE']),
 (211, 'Let us learn – II, III', [
  'II. 2. generous (clue: donated a lot) 3. help (clue: supporting him with money) 4. brightness (clue: sun’s rays came into the dark room) 5. indescribable (clue: speechless)',
  'III. 1. morrow – tomorrow / the next day 2. hither – here / to this place 3. quoth – said 4. betwixt – between 5. yonder – over there / at a distance']),
 (211, 'Let us listen (Transcript p. 226)', [
  'I. Order: 1. Ploughing of soil (2) 2. Puddling of land (4) 3. Irrigating the field (6) 4. Transplanting of seedlings (1) 5. Harvesting of paddy (5) 6. Threshing of grains (3) 7. Milling of rice (7)',
  'II. 1. (iii) Puddling the soil 2. (ii) 4–5 weeks 3. (iii) To store the rice grains for longer']),
 (212, 'Let us speak – II (Presentation on a harvest dish)', [
  'Sample – Pongali (Chakkera Pongali): Good morning to all of you. Today we, ___, ___ and ___ are here to tell you about Pongali, made during Sankranti. It is called Pongal in Tamil Nadu and Sweet Pongal elsewhere. Ingredients: new rice, moong dal, jaggery, milk, ghee, cashews, raisins, cardamom. Preparation: rice and dal are cooked in milk in a new pot; when it boils over (“pongu”), jaggery, ghee, cardamom and fried cashews are added. It is first offered to the Sun God and then served hot on a banana leaf. It tastes sweet and rich. Ritual: the pot is allowed to boil over as a sign of plenty. Memory: our whole family cooks it together in the courtyard on Sankranti morning. In conclusion, Pongali celebrates the new harvest. Thank you for listening.']),
 (213, 'Let us write – Slogan for farmers', [
  'Samples: 1. “No Farmer, No Food – Thank a Farmer Today!” 2. “Farmers Feed the Nation – Salute Their Dedication!” 3. “Every Grain on Our Plate Is a Farmer’s Gift – Respect It!” 4. “Annadata Sukhibhava – Thank You, Farmers!”']),
 (215, 'Let us write – Sensory passage (Prabhala Theertham)', [
  'Sample: I wonder to see dozens of tall, colourful Prabhas rising above the coconut grove at Jaggannathota. I am attracted by the bright silks, mirrors and flowers that decorate each totem. I am excited to watch brave young men carrying the heavy Prabhas across a deep stream and green paddy fields. I feel goosebumps as the crowd shouts with devotion. I am eager to join the procession. I have heard a variety of sounds – drums, nadaswaram, bells and joyful cries. I am unable to stop my nose from receiving the smell of camphor, flowers and wet earth. My tongue has started remembering the taste of the ariselu and pulihora from the stalls. I am unable to control myself from dancing to the drumbeats. The touch of the tall totems makes me feel blessed and proud of our culture. Finally, it was a day that made me joyous of our rich Sankranti tradition in Andhra Pradesh.']),
 (216, 'Let us explore – I, II', [
  'I. (i) Yes – ragi (finger millet) java/sankati, jonna (sorghum) rotti, sajja (pearl millet) rotti, korra (foxtail millet) rice are part of our Andhra cuisine. (ii) Booklet ideas: ragi malt, ragi dosa, jonna rotte, korra upma, sajja laddu, millet pongali. (iii) Own recipe (sample): Ragi-Jaggery Laddu – roast ragi flour in ghee, add powdered jaggery, cardamom and chopped groundnuts, shape into laddus.',
  'II. Collect Sankranti songs (gobbemma songs, haridasu keertanas, gangireddu songs) from elders and compile them into a class songbook with the teacher.']),
]
